course: dictionary
lesson: 12954
title: Words for money and cutting
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Excise is a formal word, so you will often meet it in news, money and history lessons. It has two meanings, and the words around it tell you which one you need. «The government added excise to the price of petrol» is about money: the price went up because of a tax.
explain: «The editor decided to excise the long scene from the film.» Here excise is a doing word. The clues are editor and film: someone is removing part of something. Try the swap: "cut out the long scene" still makes sense, so excise means to cut out or remove.
explain: «The surgeon excised the small lump.» This is about a doctor cutting out a growth. Ask yourself what is being done: is someone taxing something, or removing something? The sentence will tell you which meaning fits.
fact: Excise, as a noun, is a tax the government puts on certain goods made or sold in the country.
fact: Petrol, alcohol and tobacco are examples of goods that can have excise on them.
fact: Excise duty and excise tax are phrases you may see for this kind of tax.
fact: As a verb, excise means to cut something out or remove it, such as a part of a text or a lump in an operation.
fact: Excise is a formal word, so it is most common in news, money and history.
exercise: «The government added excise to the price of petrol.» In this sentence, what is excise? | A part of a text that has been cut out | A place where petrol is sold | *A tax on certain goods, such as petrol | A kind of doctor's operation
exercise: «The editor decided to excise the long scene from the film.» What does excise mean here? | *To cut out or remove | To add a tax to | To play loudly | To show on a screen
exercise: Which of these goods is most likely to have excise on it? | Bread | Chocolate biscuits | *Alcohol | Pencils
test: «The surgeon excised the small lump.» What is the surgeon doing? | *Cutting the lump out | Weighing the lump | Painting the lump | Taxing the lump
test: Excise is a formal word. Where would you most often find it? | In a shopping list for a birthday cake | *In news, money and history lessons | In a nursery rhyme | In a playground game
exam: Which two meanings does excise have? Choose the best answer. | To add sugar, and to whisper | A type of petrol, and a hot day | *A tax on goods, and to cut out or remove something | A long walk, and a loud noise
